.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd September 30, 2026
.Dt CHMOD 1
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm chmod
.Nd change file modes
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl fhv
.Op Fl R Op Fl H | L | P
.Ar mode
.Ar file ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
sets the permission bits of each
.Ar file
as
.Ar mode
says.
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl f
Don't report files that can't be changed (the exit status still says so).
.It Fl h
Change a symbolic link itself, not what it points to, where the system can;
otherwise leave it alone.
.It Fl R
Change the files in each directory, and in the directories in it, too.
Symbolic links met on the way are not followed
.Pq Fl P ,
unless
.Fl H
(follow those named on the command line) or
.Fl L
(follow all) is given; the last of the three counts.
.It Fl v
Name each file changed; given twice, also show its old and new mode.
.El
.Ss Modes
A mode is either octal, a number from 0 to 7777 made of these:
.Bl -column "XXXX" "set-group-ID" "0400, 0200, 0100" "owner's read, write, execute" -offset indent -compact
.It 4000 Ta set-user-ID Ta 0400, 0200, 0100 Ta owner's read, write, execute
.It 2000 Ta set-group-ID Ta 0040, 0020, 0010 Ta group's
.It 1000 Ta sticky Ta 0004, 0002, 0001 Ta others'
.El
.Pp
or symbolic: clauses separated by commas, each who it is for, then one or more
operations.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Who
.Cm u
the owner,
.Cm g
the group,
.Cm o
others,
.Cm a
all three.
With none, all three are meant, but bits set in the
.Xr umask 2
are neither added nor removed.
.It Operation
.Cm +
adds the permissions that follow,
.Cm -
removes them,
.Cm =
clears the classes' bits (with no
.Cm who ,
all bits) and then adds them.
.It Permissions
.Cm r ,
.Cm w ,
.Cm x ;
.Cm X ,
execute only for a directory or a file that someone may already execute;
.Cm s ,
set-user-ID with
.Cm u
and set-group-ID with
.Cm g ;
.Cm t ,
the sticky bit, with
.Cm o ,
.Cm a
or no
.Cm who .
Or one of
.Cm u ,
.Cm g
or
.Cm o
alone: a copy of that class's current permissions.
.El
.Pp
Operations apply left to right, each to the result of the one before.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bl -tag -width "u=rwx,go=rx" -compact
.It Li 644
owner reads and writes, everyone else reads
.It Li u+x
add the owner's execute
.It Li go-w
nobody but the owner may write
.It Li g=u
the group gets what the owner has
.It Li a+rX
everyone may read, and search directories
.It Li u=rwx,go=rx
the same as 755
.El
.Pp
Make a tree private to its owner:
.Dl chmod -R go-rwx project
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ls 1 ,
.Xr chmod 2 ,
.Xr umask 2
.Sh STANDARDS
.Nm
conforms to
.St -p1003.1-2008 ;
.Fl f ,
.Fl h ,
.Fl v ,
.Fl H ,
.Fl L
and
.Fl P
are extensions, as on the BSDs.
.Sh HISTORY
A
.Nm
command appeared in
.At v1 .
